// Field reference: https://github.com/peterellisjones/dronecom-community/blob/main/data/docs/components.md
//
// Surface-class active repeater (#5171) — the in-game Nulka, and the epic's
// phantom-fleet payload: a cheap boat returning a warship's cross-section. Like
// every repeater it is silent until painted, so the fleet that lights it up is
// the fleet that believes it.
//
// The 40 kW draw is what makes this a *surface* class, structurally rather than
// by declaration: it exceeds the largest air chassis's spare supply
// (`power_supply_kw - base_power_draw_kw`; the MQ-450's 29.0 kW is the ceiling)
// by 1.38x, so `Blueprint::validate` rejects it on every air hull with
// `OverPowerBudget`. That exclusion is asserted over the whole shipped chassis
// registry, so a new airframe is covered the moment it lands.
//
// Unlike the 60 kW AN/SLQ-80, this class deliberately fits the USV-80 (45.0 kW
// spare), because a cheap hull radiating a warship's return is the entire play —
// on the USV-200 the arithmetic is far less interesting. It leaves that boat
// exactly 5.0 kW, which buys an AN/ALR-20 threat receiver and nothing else: a
// phantom that knows when it is being painted is buildable, a phantom that is
// also a warship is not.
//
// `calibration_range_m` of 40 km puts the signature-capable unmask range at
// 20047 m (see the AN/ALE-35 for the arithmetic). That is chosen against sea
// engagement geometry: an an_sps_90 classifies a portrayed CV-1000 as a carrier
// from 65.8 km and an an_spy_310 from 86.9 km, both comfortably outside
// anti-ship release ranges, so the lie survives the LAUNCH DECISION — which is
// what the payload is for — and dies at 20 km, where an an_spy_310 can also
// identify the true USV-80 (27.5 km). It is 4x the air class's value, which is
// also what keeps the two classes mutually non-dominated in the decoy dominance
// scan; a retune has to preserve that ordering.
//
// Every shipped sea hull is Screw, so propulsion plausibility is automatic here.
// The portrayal survives the kinematic channels too, and deliberately:
// `NtdsClass::CommandShip` shares `BUCKET_SURFACE` with `NtdsClass::Surface`, so
// a USV portraying a carrier is not penalised on kin bucket, and the stall-speed
// floor is inert for non-FixedWing portrayals. The surface lie is therefore
// broken only by the repeater anomaly above or by EO/visual truth up close.
//
// cv_3000 is deliberately absent: one carrier rung. Letting an ~$8M delivered
// phantom conjure the most valuable hull class in the game leaves no rung above
// it to make the choice interesting. Nothing in the sim would object — this is
// an authoring decision, and a reversible one.
//
// ORDER IS LIVE GAMEPLAY: the first entry is what this payload portrays with no
// doctrine selection. cv_1000 leads because the phantom carrier is the play.
// usv_200 is the second rung and the cheapest entry, so it is the one that binds
// the substitution guard.
(
    id: "an_slq_95",
    designator: "AN/SLQ-95",
    codename: "Leviathan",
    category: Decoys,
    mount_rule: Single,
    // Heavier than the 150 kg AN/SLQ-80 while drawing less, and both for the
    // same reason: a repeater adds a receiver front-end to the transmit chain,
    // but only has to amplify a pulse the victim radar delivered, where a
    // spoofer must shout a shipboard array's rated power into the band unaided.
    weight: 200.0,
    // See the class-gate note above. Authored, never derived.
    power_draw: Fixed(40.0),
    // Unrestricted on purpose: the power budget does the excluding, which is
    // what the issue asks for. `[Domain(Sea)]` is the documented backstop if an
    // air chassis ever fields a 40 kW supply — nothing close exists today.
    allowed: [],
    payload: Decoy((
        channel: RadarRepeater(
            emulates: ["cv_1000", "usv_200"],
            calibration_range_m: 40000.0,
        ),
    )),
)
